00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:05.330
We've been following all of the different
possible paths forward for treating the virus.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:08.000
We've talked about antiviral of antivirals.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.740
We've talked about antibody treatments.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:13.760
We've talked about the path to a vaccine every
time that this comes up.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:19.610
I am careful to say these are things that
are being considered in early testing.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:20.940
We need to know more.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.360
We don't have much data yet.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:28.850
I now have for you today, and there is a degree
to which we we are sort of on the on the vanguard

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:32.300
here of of the treatment of this.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:36.899
We now have promising results from not one,
but two of these studies.

00:00:36.899 --> 00:00:43.229
And we're also learning a lot more about possible
predispositions to getting a more serious

00:00:43.229 --> 00:00:44.269
case of the virus.

00:00:44.269 --> 00:00:47.979
We'll talk about that as well based on blood
type, some early data about that.

00:00:47.979 --> 00:00:48.979
So let's jump right in.

00:00:48.979 --> 00:00:50.559
First of all, is Avogadro's OK?

00:00:50.559 --> 00:00:55.600
I told you about AV again earlier this week,
AV against the Japanese antiviral.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.530
It was developed by a subsidiary of Fujifilm.

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:00.649
If you can believe that back in 2014.

00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:06.299
The idea was the Japanese government wanted
to have something to stockpile against a serious

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:09.520
flu epidemic at some point in the future.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.920
They said this is as good a time as any to
test it on coronavirus.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:18.159
And for a while there was mere speculation
that AV again may be effective.

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:23.539
We now have a clinical trial involving 340
Chinese patients and the results are that

00:01:23.539 --> 00:01:30.229
AV again is turning people negative for the
virus after a median of just four days compared

00:01:30.229 --> 00:01:33.729
with a median of eleven days for people who
did not get the drug.

00:01:33.729 --> 00:01:39.890
We've heard it can take weeks for the body
to fight this naturally median seems to be

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:41.010
11 days.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:44.399
That means half take less than eleven days
have take more than eleven days.

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:48.390
AV again reduces the median to four days.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:52.200
Lungs X-rays of the lungs improved within
four days.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:57.630
In ninety one percent of the people taking
AB again, compared with just 62 percent sort

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:01.740
of fighting the virus naturally without assistance,
so to speak.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:07.039
The results also say the safety of the drug
in terms of side effects seems high.

00:02:07.039 --> 00:02:09.180
That's been a concern with Afghan.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.729
Still need more information there.

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:12.420
So that's all the good news.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:16.560
The thing to keep in mind about the Afghan
trials so far is that they seem to really

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.370
only work well for people with mild to moderate
cases.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:25.370
If you have a case where it's more serious
and the virus is already well established,

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:28.049
the Afghan does not seem to work particularly
well.

00:02:28.049 --> 00:02:32.760
So this could possibly be approved as a coronavirus
treatment by May.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.230
And that's very interesting.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.689
Second, and this is even more exciting in
my mind.

00:02:37.689 --> 00:02:41.290
I told you about the French biologist and
medical doctor.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:47.770
In other words, he's an M.D. and a biology
p._h._d DDA Rao, and he's been doing some

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:49.610
very good small trials.

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:54.629
He has now published very good results from
a trial about chloroquine.

00:02:54.629 --> 00:02:58.030
I mentioned chloroquine as a possible treatment.

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:01.430
Chloroquine is a really old cheap anti-malaria
drug.

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:03.820
It's been around since 1934.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:08.850
It's very inexpensive and it has shown some
some promise in treating the virus.

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:16.630
Dr. Rao found in a small trial that a 600
milligram per day dose of hydroxy chloroquine,

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:23.180
which is a version of the drug, reduced the
viral load in swab tests significantly.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.349
That's great by itself.

00:03:25.349 --> 00:03:27.060
But now it's getting even more interesting.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:34.220
Dr. Ruth did an additional test combining
the hydroxy chloroquine dose with a zero Meissen.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:40.599
Now, if a Cythera Myson sounds familiar to
you, that's an antibiotic widely available,

00:03:40.599 --> 00:03:44.280
sold under the sort of marketing name of Zithromax.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.870
I took it years ago for a sinus infection.

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:51.920
It's very commonly taken for a number of different
bacterial infections.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:57.959
Combining a zip throw Meissen with chloroquine
in Dr. Rao's studies seems to be even better

00:03:57.959 --> 00:03:58.959
now.

00:03:58.959 --> 00:03:59.959
I know what you're going to say.

00:03:59.959 --> 00:04:01.120
Well done, David.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.970
This is a virus, not a bacteria.

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:05.319
Antibiotics work on bacteria.

00:04:05.319 --> 00:04:09.530
Why on earth would a zero myson help a zit
throw?

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:15.550
Myson, despite being an antibiotic, has been
